.\" Generated by "sysml-jupyter-kernel -man"; edit the description in the command's usage.go.
.TH SYSML-JUPYTER-KERNEL 1 "2026-08-30" "OpenSysML" "OpenSysML Manual"
.SH NAME
sysml\-jupyter\-kernel \- serve SysML v2 to Jupyter notebooks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysml\-jupyter\-kernel
\-connection\-file FILE [\-verbose]
.br
.B sysml\-jupyter\-kernel
\-install [\-user | \-prefix DIR] [\-name NAME] [\-display\-name NAME]
.br
.B sysml\-jupyter\-kernel
\-print\-kernelspec
.br
.B sysml\-jupyter\-kernel
\-version | \-man
.SH DESCRIPTION
sysml\-jupyter\-kernel is a Jupyter kernel over the OpenSysML REPL session: a
notebook cell is read as the prompt reads lines, so declarations accumulate
into one session model, a bare expression is evaluated, and every % command of
the sysml REPL runs as it does there. Views rendered as Mermaid, Markdown, DOT
or tables are shown in that form by the front end; everything else prints as
the prompt prints it.
.PP
Jupyter starts the kernel itself, passing \-connection\-file; \-install writes
the kernelspec that tells it how. Interrupting a cell stops the run it drives
at its next step.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-connection\-file " string"
Serve the connection Jupyter describes in this file (Jupyter passes it)
.TP
.BI \-display\-name " string"
With \-install, the name front ends list the kernel under. The default is
SysML v2 (OpenSysML).
.TP
.B \-install
Write the kernelspec that starts this binary where Jupyter finds kernels, and
exit
.TP
.B \-man
Write this command's manual page, in roff, to stdout and exit
.TP
.BI \-name " string"
With \-install, the kernelspec's directory name, which notebooks bind to. The
default is sysml.
.TP
.BI \-prefix " string"
With \-install, install under this prefix (PREFIX/share/jupyter/kernels), as
into a virtual environment
.TP
.B \-print\-kernelspec
Write the kernel.json \-install would write to stdout, and exit
.TP
.B \-user
With \-install, install for this user alone (the default without \-prefix)
.TP
.B \-verbose
Log the protocol traffic that is dropped or fails to stderr
.TP
.B \-version
Show version and exit
.SH INSTALLING
.RS 2
.nf
# Register the kernel for this user
sysml\-jupyter\-kernel \-install
# Register it in a virtual environment
sysml\-jupyter\-kernel \-install \-prefix "$VIRTUAL_ENV"
# Show the kernel.json a package would ship
sysml\-jupyter\-kernel \-print\-kernelspec
# See that Jupyter finds it
jupyter kernelspec list
.fi
.RE
.PP
The kernelspec names this binary by its absolute path, so moving the binary
needs another \-install. The pip and conda package jupyter\-opensysml\-kernel
installs a kernelspec of its own, holding the binary, so it needs no
\-install.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The status reports how the kernel ended:
.TP
.B 0
The front end shut it down, or it reported what was asked of it.
.TP
.B 1
It could not serve: a channel could not be bound.
.TP
.B 2
The command line or the connection file could not be acted on.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_LIBRARY_PATH
Directory to load the SysML/KerML standard library from, instead of the copy
embedded in the binary.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_STEPS
Expression evaluations one run may spend before it is reported as a runaway.
Default 10000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_ACTION_STEPS
Token\-flow steps one action run may perform. Default 1000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_EVENTS
Events one state machine run may dispatch. Default 1000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_DO_STEPS
Do actions one state machine run may perform. Default 5000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_ELEMENTS
Collection elements one evaluation may hold, which bounds the memory a run
holds rather than the work it does. Default 1000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_CALC_DEPTH
Nested calc invocations one run may hold on the stack, which is what a
recursion spends. Default 10000, ceiling 25000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_SWEEP_RUNS
Runs one parameter sweep or sample may make, each a whole analysis or calc run
of its own with its own budgets. Default 1000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_INTEGER_BITS
Bits the magnitude of one Integer a run computes may take; Integers are
otherwise unbounded, and a result past it, such as a large power, is refused
before it is computed. At least 64, so every machine\-word Integer fits;
default 1048576.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_JOBS
Runs of one check that may go concurrently \(em the linearizations of an
exploration, the engines \-engine all consults \(em each on a worker of its
own over the shared model; \-jobs and %jobs override it. Default one per CPU,
fewer where the memory available leaves less than 512 MiB per worker.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_TOOLS
Directory of the tool manifest: one JSON file per external tool (toolName,
version, executable, variables), each registered as the engine tool:<name>
that runs actions and calcs annotated ToolExecution with that toolName. Unset
registers no tool, and such an action or calc is refused.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_TOOL_TIMEOUT
How long one tool invocation may take, as a Go duration. Default 10s, after
which the performance or calculation fails.
.PP
Each variable above also answers to its legacy SYSML_\-prefixed name
(SYSML_MAX_STEPS for OPENSYSML_MAX_STEPS, and so on), which remains accepted.
When both are set and the OPENSYSML_ value is non\-empty, the OPENSYSML_ value
wins; setting only the legacy name prints a one\-time deprecation warning on
standard error.
.PP
A budget bounds one run \(em one evaluation, one instantiation, one calc
invocation, one action, one state machine \(em not a whole session, so a long
session of small operations never exhausts one. A run started inside another
shares the outer run's budget.
